5. What is Routing?

Routing determines which endpoint/controller/action should handle an incoming HTTP request.

Example:

GET /api/products/10

can map to:

[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult GetProduct(int id)
{
}
Attribute Routing
[Route("api/products")]
public class ProductController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult Get(int id)
    {
        return Ok();
    }
}
Conventional Routing
{controller}/{action}/{id?}

Example:

/Product/Get/10
Interview Answer

Routing maps an incoming URL and HTTP method to a specific controller action or endpoint.

6. What are Filters?

Filters are components in ASP.NET Core MVC that allow us to execute code at specific stages of request processing.

Common filters:

Authorization Filter
Resource Filter
Action Filter
Exception Filter
Result Filter
Example
public class LogActionFilter : IActionFilter
{
    public void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Before action");
    }

    public void OnActionExecuted(ActionExecutedContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("After action");
    }
}
Middleware vs Filter
Middleware	Filter
HTTP pipeline	MVC pipeline
Works for all requests	Mostly MVC/controller actions
Authentication, logging, CORS etc.	Action-level concerns
Runs before MVC	Runs around controller/action execution
7. What is an Action Filter?

An Action Filter executes code before and/or after a controller action.

Example:

public class LoggingFilter : IActionFilter
{
    public void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Action started");
    }

    public void OnActionExecuted(ActionExecutedContext context)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Action completed");
    }
}

Register:

builder.Services.AddControllers(options =>
{
    options.Filters.Add<LoggingFilter>();
});
Use Cases
Logging
Validation
Auditing
Measuring execution time
Common action-level logic
8. How do you handle Global Exception Handling?

Instead of putting:

try
{
}
catch(Exception ex)
{
}

inside every controller, we can handle exceptions globally.

Using built-in middleware
if (!app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseExceptionHandler("/error");
}

For APIs, we can also create custom exception middleware.

app.UseMiddleware<ExceptionMiddleware>();

Example:

public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
{
    try
    {
        await _next(context);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        context.Response.StatusCode = 500;

        await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new
        {
            message = "Something went wrong"
        });
    }
}
Interview Answer

I prefer centralized exception handling using ASP.NET Core exception-handling middleware so that controllers remain clean and errors can be logged and returned in a consistent format.

9. What is async/await? Why is it used?

async and await are used for asynchronous programming.

They allow a thread to avoid waiting/blocking while an I/O operation is running.

Example:

public async Task<Product> GetProduct()
{
    return await _context.Products.FirstOrDefaultAsync();
}

Instead of:

var product = _context.Products.FirstOrDefault();
Common use cases
Database calls
API calls
File operations
Network operations
Important

async/await does not automatically mean creating a new thread.

For I/O operations, it allows the thread to be released while waiting.

10. Difference Between Task and Thread
Thread	Task
Actual execution thread	Represents asynchronous work
More expensive	Lightweight
Manually manages thread	Usually uses ThreadPool
Low-level	Higher-level abstraction
Thread.Sleep()	await Task.Delay()
Thread
Thread thread = new Thread(() =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("Running");
});

thread.Start();
Task
Task.Run(() =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("Running");
});
Interview Answer

Thread is an actual execution thread managed by the OS/runtime, while Task is a higher-level abstraction representing asynchronous work. Tasks are generally preferred for modern .NET asynchronous programming.

11. IEnumerable vs IQueryable

This is very important for EF Core interviews.

IEnumerable

Works mainly with data that is already in memory.

IEnumerable<Product> products =
    context.Products.ToList();

var result = products.Where(x => x.Price > 100);

The filtering happens in memory.

IQueryable

Builds a query that can be translated and executed by the database provider.

IQueryable<Product> products =
    context.Products;

var result = products
    .Where(x => x.Price > 100)
    .ToList();

The filtering can become SQL:

SELECT *
FROM Products
WHERE Price > 100;
Main Difference
IEnumerable
    ↓
Data comes to application
    ↓
Filtering in memory

IQueryable
    ↓
Query sent to database
    ↓
Database performs filtering
Interview Point

IQueryable is generally useful with EF Core when we want filtering, sorting, projection etc. to be executed by the database rather than loading unnecessary data into memory.

