15. Explain JWT Authentication. Where is the Token Stored?

JWT = JSON Web Token.

Typical flow:

Client
   ↓
Login credentials
   ↓
Server
   ↓
Validate user
   ↓
Generate JWT
   ↓
Client

Then client sends:

Authorization: Bearer <token>
JWT Structure
Header.Payload.Signature

Example:

xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz
Server

ASP.NET Core validates:

Signature
Expiration
Issuer
Audience
Where is JWT stored?

It depends on the application.

For browser applications, common approaches include:

Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies
In-memory application state
Browser storage such as localStorage is possible but exposes the token to JavaScript, so an XSS vulnerability can make it risky.
Interview Answer

JWT is a stateless authentication mechanism where the server issues a signed token after successful login. The client sends the token with subsequent requests, commonly using the Authorization Bearer header or a secure HttpOnly cookie.

16. Why is DbContext Registered as Scoped?

Usually:

builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(
    options => ...
);

AddDbContext() registers DbContext as Scoped by default.

That means:

HTTP Request
     ↓
One DbContext
     ↓
Repositories/Services
     ↓
HTTP Response

Each request gets its own DbContext instance.

Why Scoped?

DbContext:

Is not thread-safe
Represents a unit of work
Tracks entity changes
Should generally be short-lived
Interview Answer

DbContext is normally scoped because we want one context per HTTP request/unit of work, while avoiding sharing the same DbContext across concurrent requests.

