31. Explain SOLID Principles

SOLID contains five principles.

S — Single Responsibility Principle

A class should have one primary responsibility and therefore one main reason to change.

Bad:

class Employee
{
    void CalculateSalary() {}
    void SaveToDatabase() {}
    void SendEmail() {}
}

Better:

EmployeeService
SalaryService
EmployeeRepository
EmailService
O — Open/Closed Principle

Open for extension, closed for modification.

You should be able to add new behavior without constantly modifying existing working code.

Example:

interface IPayment
{
    void Pay();
}

Implement:

class CreditCardPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay() {}
}

class UpiPayment : IPayment
{
    public void Pay() {}
}

Add another payment type without changing existing implementations.

L — Liskov Substitution Principle

A child class should be usable wherever its parent type is expected without breaking the expected behavior.

Example concept:

Bird
  ↓
FlyingBird

Not every bird can necessarily fly, so forcing every Bird to have Fly() can violate LSP.

I — Interface Segregation Principle

Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they don't use.

Bad:

interface IWorker
{
    void Work();
    void Eat();
    void Sleep();
}

Better:

interface IWorkable
{
    void Work();
}

interface IEatable
{
    void Eat();
}
D — Dependency Inversion Principle

High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.

Bad:

class OrderService
{
    private PaymentService _payment = new PaymentService();
}

Better:

class OrderService
{
    private readonly IPaymentService _payment;

    public OrderService(IPaymentService payment)
    {
        _payment = payment;
    }
}
SOLID Memory Trick
S → Single Responsibility
O → Open/Closed
L → Liskov Substitution
I → Interface Segregation
D → Dependency Inversion
32. What is API Throttling?

API throttling controls how frequently a client can call an API.

Example:

User → API

100 requests/minute → Allowed

101st request
     ↓
429 Too Many Requests

It protects APIs from:

Excessive traffic
Abuse
Resource exhaustion
Accidental request spikes
Example

Suppose:

100 requests/minute/user

If the user sends 150 requests:

First 100 → Processed
Remaining → Throttled

HTTP status:

429 Too Many Requests
Throttling vs Rate Limiting

These terms are often used together, but conceptually:

Rate limiting defines the allowed request rate, while throttling is the act of restricting/slowing requests when that limit is exceeded.
